<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sisavang Vong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1885">1885</a>–<a href="/years/1959">1959</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sisavang Vong was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Phrabang</a> from 1904 under French protection and King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> from 1946 until his death in 1959. In 1945 he sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> against the Lao Issara movement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phetsarath-ratanavongsa">Prince Phetsarath</a>, and under him Laos became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-union">French Union</a>, gaining full independence in 1953.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sisavang-vong.jpg" alt="Sisavang Vong" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified author of the publication (photographers, designers, etc. not credited). … · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roi_Sisavang_Vong_(crop_3x4).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sisavang Vong was born as Prince Khao on 14 July 1885 in the Golden Palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a>, the eldest surviving son of King Zakarine and Queen Consort Thong-sy. His father reigned there under French colonial authority. As a child he studied at the Lycee Chasseloup-Laubat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> and afterwards at the Ecole Coloniale, and then returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>.</p><p>His father died on 26 March 1904, and Khao became heir apparent on 15 April 1904. He ascended the throne at the Old Royal Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a> and was crowned on 4 March 1905, taking the courtesy name Sisavangvong in keeping with Lao tradition. He was the last ruler of the Lao Kingdom of Luang Prabang and the founding king of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In the early years of his reign the French built the Royal Palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a> as his residence. He united several provinces under his authority: Houaphan in 1931, Houakhong, Xiengkhouang and Vientiane in 1942, and Champassak and Sayboury in 1946. Throughout his reign he supported the French administration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>.</p><p>In late 1945 he briefly ruled as king of a Japanese puppet state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a>, but he refused to collaborate with Lao nationalists, and the Lao Issara removed him from power for his pro-French stance when they declared the nation's independence. The French regained control in April 1946 and restored him as king. It was the first time in almost 250 years that a Lao monarch ruled the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>. His reign lasted 55 years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When he fell ill Sisavang Vong appointed his son, Crown Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savang-vatthana">Savang Vatthana</a>, as regent, and the son succeeded him after his death in 1959. He was cremated and buried at Vat That Luang in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a> in 1961. The funeral procession included a royal funeral carriage, a wooden hearse 12 metres high decorated with a carved seven-headed snake, and many dignitaries attended the state funeral, among them Prince Bhanubandhu Yugala, who represented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. Sisavangvong University was founded in his honour but was closed in 1975, when the communist regime took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>. Statues of him survive in Luang Prabang and Vientiane, showing him giving the people a constitution.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sisavang Vong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 9 March 1945 the Japanese overthrew French rule in a coup, interning French troops and officials, and encouraged Bao Dai in Vietnam, Norodom Sihanouk in Cambodia and <b>Sisavang Vong</b> in Laos to declare independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1946 France and King <b>Sisavang Vong</b> agreed to unite Luang Phrabang, Vientiane, Champasak and other Lao provinces into a single kingdom, with Prince Boun Oum of Champasak renouncing his claims.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-laos">Kingdom of Laos</a></li><li><blockquote>Its king <b>Sisavang Vong</b> became monarch of the unified Kingdom of Laos in 1946 to 1947.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-luang-phrabang">Kingdom of Luang Phrabang</a></li><li><blockquote>When King <b>Sisavang Vong</b> sided with France and dismissed him, Phetsarath's supporters formed the Lao Issara government in Vientiane in October 1945 and deposed the king.</blockquote><a href="/state/lao-issara">Lao Issara</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/laos">Laos</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/laos">Laos</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.89,102.14&amp;year=1933" aria-label="Luang Prabang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7306 1427 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sisavang Vong"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7756 1697h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7756 1697h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.89,102.14&amp;year=1933">What was here in 1933? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
